#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562664 :question-generator/id #uuid "92c30f27-b80e-57fb-9e00-3c9574502db6" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186124595.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a66db9c-0ec7-5bff-93d3-e61113901baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478640} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6ab715-8c58-5119-aca6-445e2d5a78d0" :answer/t