substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(- 4 r)\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =- 4 r \\sin {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d346b61-2975-5e5f-9fa8-5fce8af784ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3) \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562605} {:answer/id #uuid "28f23e07-5647-548b-9fc4-9f74ff4d8268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{3} \\sin {3 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta} - 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3) \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562613} {:answer/id #uuid "2f236631-d38f-52c8-8607-34a183d9caab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3) \\sin {\\theta} - 6 r^{2} \\sin {3 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562615} {:answer/id #uuid "627bff0f-b141-50c5-8d14-e2bb149c2d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\cos {3 \\theta} + 18 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} + 6 r^{2} \\sin {3 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562626} {:answer/id #uuid "b08d99de-b096-5b26-81b0-bb5463a7b183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3) \\cos {\\theta} - 6 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\sin {3 \\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "627bff0f-b141-50c5-8d14-e2bb149c2d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\cos {3 \\theta} + 18 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} + 6 r^{2} \\sin {3 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562626} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f98e735-fc04-5a66-8a08-e78543b54d1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d42128d-ec23-537b-8f25-68833c35b58e" :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta)=- 2 r^{3} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3)$, find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta}\\cos \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3),\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta}=6 r^{3} \\sin {3 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(- 6 r^{2} (\\cos {3 \\theta} - 3))\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}(6 r^{3} \\sin {3 \\theta})\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =18{r}^{2}\\sin \\theta-6{r}^{2}\\cos 3\\theta\\sin \\theta+6{r}^{2}\\sin 3\\theta\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =-2{r}^{2}(-9\\sin \\theta+3\\cos 3\\theta\\sin \\theta-3\\sin 3\\theta\\cos \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "250dcc11-6c14-5c7a-8ca0-4baf461295c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562609} {:answer/id #uuid "4deadc9a-b2f2-567a-a980-49db2c37067b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562622} {:answer/id #uuid "7e03a989-2920-54d8-812f-4dcff83c2898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562635} {:answer/id #uuid "877fcc0c-4b48-5a6e-9f12-8460cbd39891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562640} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcce618-3d7a-51a2-ad85-eb6bde2a4ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 r \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "250dcc11-6c14-5c7a-8ca0-4baf461295c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562609} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ba1694-6948-5d59-ab5c-f346f0b0efa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78de899d-6101-5c4a-ac37-a4bcf9390397" :question/problem "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=r^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=2 r,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(2 r)\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =2 r \\cos {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257b2611-dea9-554b-a87c-5ec9d708e4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3) \\sin {\\theta} - 6 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta} \\cos {2 \\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562610} {:answer/id #uuid "42d46fc2-7c72-5bcc-98bc-5b71a4c7ec3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3) \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562618} {:answer/id #uuid "773b4f4b-020a-580b-bd4a-1d3c88396584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{3} \\sin {\\theta} \\cos {2 \\theta} - 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3) \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562633} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d6f9a0-a166-5c58-8d6a-45929391b7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3) \\cos {\\theta} - 6 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\cos {2 \\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562654} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7e3124-a995-52a8-85a4-7c30ae0621f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\cos {2 \\theta} - 9 r^{2} \\sin {2 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta} + 27 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc7e3124-a995-52a8-85a4-7c30ae0621f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\cos {2 \\theta} - 9 r^{2} \\sin {2 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta} + 27 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562663} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5940c0-a5be-51ef-8985-5607309df90d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fa41182-3b3f-56dc-b005-91fd22936911" :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=- 3 r^{3} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3)$, find $\\frac{∂u}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}=- 6 r^{3} \\cos {2 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =(- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(- 6 r^{3} \\cos {2 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =27{r}^{2}\\cos \\theta-9{r}^{2}\\sin 2\\theta\\cos \\theta+6{r}^{2}\\cos 2\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-3{r}^{2}(-9\\cos \\theta+3\\sin 2\\theta\\cos \\theta-2\\cos 2\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18656e36-b360-596c-906f-c6142433c6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 r \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562604} {:answer/id #uuid "246038ff-e08b-58fe-a4b1-bafce1919350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 r \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562608} {:answer/id #uuid "586adb3d-8dc3-5c10-b04c-05dd6518dacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 r \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562624} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4350a9-7085-544f-bc6e-0361174ad019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562644} {:answer/id #uuid "e4493ba7-e108-5a71-9992-4b9775de69a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586adb3d-8dc3-5c10-b04c-05dd6518dacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 r \\sin {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562624} :answer-field/id #uuid "69718cef-56a9-51cb-be04-46c69783f886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d17b8964-d9e1-548f-aaee-6885acfaabe4" :question/problem "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=- 4 r^{2}$, find $\\frac{∂u}{∂y}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=\\frac{∂u}{∂r}\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}\\cos \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂r}=- 8 r,\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =(- 8 r)\\sin \\theta+\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\cos \\theta \\\\ & =- 8 r \\sin {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23cf110c-663a-5575-9e31-02a4d579faa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 r^{2} \\sin {\\theta} \\sin {4 \\theta} - 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1) \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562607} {:answer/id #uuid "259595a0-2ade-545f-a053-58ef37924e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1) \\cos {\\theta} + 12 r \\sin {\\theta} \\sin {4 \\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562611} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8ee3b3-2fd7-505a-80dc-de1633ff6ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1) \\sin {\\theta} + 12 r \\sin {4 \\theta} \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562621} {:answer/id #uuid "c3498915-bc44-52ae-84f3-0f8b3feecf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1) \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562650} {:answer/id #uuid "da4b3e6f-897f-5e04-9f89-096ca0cb0b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 r \\sin {\\theta} \\sin {4 \\theta} - 6 r \\cos {\\theta} \\cos {4 \\theta} + 6 r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da4b3e6f-897f-5e04-9f89-096ca0cb0b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 r \\sin {\\theta} \\sin {4 \\theta} - 6 r \\cos {\\theta} \\cos {4 \\theta} + 6 r \\cos {\\theta}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562655} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5e8d72-bdd0-5187-905e-6a45c80fb3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0ca78ec-e0f9-58c4-84d3-2a3878a02883" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta)=- 3 r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1)$, find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1),\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta}=12 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(- 6 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 1))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(12 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =6r\\cos \\theta-6r\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta-12r\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-3r(-2\\cos \\theta+2\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+4\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}]}
